After parsing, BeautifulSoup builds a tree of objects:

<html>
└──

<head>
      │ └── <title> → .text = "VT Poddar"
            └──

<body>
      └── <h1> → .text = "Hi"

            Now you can query the tree:

            soup.title.text # "VT Poddar"
            soup.find("h1").text # "Hi"
            soup.find_all("a") # all <a> tags

            Analogy: Fetching is like downloading a PDF.
            Parsing is like opening it and reading specific paragraphs instead of scrolling through raw bytes.



How easyprivacy and adblockparser work 

The pipeline
Browser session → Network requests → [Tracker identification] → Compare with policy text → Report gaps

EasyPrivacy is your tracker knowledge base. When PolicyTrace observes a request to
google-analytics.com, facebook.net, doubleclick.net, etc., it needs to answer: "Is this a known
tracker, and what category is it?" The EasyPrivacy list encodes exactly that — each rule has a
domain/URL pattern plus an optional comment identifying the service and its purpose (analytics,
advertising, fingerprinting, etc.). You can parse the list into a structured database:

{ domain: "google-analytics.com", service: "Google Analytics", category: "analytics" }
{ domain: "doubleclick.net", service: "Google Ads", category: "advertising" }

This becomes the "what is this?" lookup when a request fires.

adblockparser is your matching engine. A single page load can generate 50–200+ network requests. You
need to check each one against ~100k filter rules in real time. The tokenization + domain-index
approach means you only do expensive regex matching on the small subset of rules that could possibly
match a given URL. Without it, you'd be doing 100k regex checks per request, which would make the tool
unusable.

Regex is the pattern primitive underneath both. Each EasyPrivacy rule like ||google-analytics.com^
gets compiled into a regex. The adblockparser optimizes how those regexes are applied, but the rules
themselves are regex patterns.



## What "Naive split" means

It's the **lazy shortcut** people take instead of using tldextract:

```python
# Naive split: just take the last two dot-parts
domain = "shop.bbc.co.uk"
parts  = domain.split(".")
naive  = ".".join(parts[-2:])   # → "co.uk"  ❌ WRONG
```

They assume: *"A domain is always `something.tld` — last two parts = the real domain."*

That works for `google.com` → `google.com` ✅. But it **breaks** when the TLD itself has dots:

| URL | Naive split (last 2 parts) | Correct (tldextract) |
|-----|:---:|:---:|
| `shop.bbc.co.uk` | `co.uk` ❌ | `bbc.co.uk` ✅ |
| `mail.gov.in` | `gov.in` ❌ | `gov.in` ✅ *(this one happens to work)* |
| `a.b.gov.in` | `gov.in` ❌ | `a.gov.in` ✅ |
| `cdn.example.com.au` | `com.au` ❌ | `example.com.au` ✅ |

**Why it matters for you:** If you're auditing `a.b.gov.in` and a request goes to `c.b.gov.in`, naive split says both are `gov.in` → "same domain" → you'd wrongly call it first-party. tldextract correctly says `a.gov.in` ≠ `c.gov.in` → different organizations → third-party.

## The whole thing in one sentence

> **tldextract** tells you *"where does this domain actually end?"* by consulting the **Public Suffix List** (a maintained list of all multi-part TLDs like `.co.uk`, `.gov.in`, `.com.au`). **Naive split** just guesses "last two parts" and gets it wrong whenever the TLD has a dot in it.

```python
import tldextract

ext = tldextract.extract("shop.bbc.co.uk")
ext.domain            # "shop"
ext.suffix            # "co.uk"      ← the actual TLD (has a dot!)
ext.registered_domain # "bbc.co.uk"  ← what you compare for first/third-party
```

That's the only reason you need it: to get `registered_domain` correctly, so your first-party/third-party check doesn't misclassify `.co.uk`, `.gov.in`, `.ac.in`, `.com.au` sites.

